  <content>
  <section><title>Spotting the Error When Solving Quadratics</title>
<para>To find the error when solving quadratics, it is helpful to first solve the problem on your own.</para>
<para>Solve the quadratic equation by <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term> and finding its zeros. Identify the error in the worked solution below.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{ll} x^{2}+10 x-34=0 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}+10 x=34 &amp; \text { Step 2 } \\ x^{2}+10 x+25=34-25 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \\ (x+5)^{2}=9 &amp; \text { Step 4 } \\ x+5=\pm \; 3 &amp; \text { Step 5 } \\ x=-8, x=-2 &amp; \text { Step 6 } \end{array} \)</para>
<para>Now, find the error by working through the problem and comparing steps:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{ll} x^{2}+10 x-34=0 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}+10 x=34 &amp; \text { Step 2 } \\ x^{2}+10 x+25=34+25 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \end{array} \)</para>
<para>In Step 3, 25 was added to the left side of the equation, but 25 was SUBTRACTED from the right side of the equation. Equations must remain balanced, and 25 should be added to each side of the equation.</para>
<title>Try It: Spotting the Error When Solving Quadratics</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Examine the steps below and identify the error in solving the quadratic equation by completing the square:</para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{lcc}x^2+8x+7=0&amp;\text{ Step 1 }&amp; \\ x^2+8x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 2 }&amp; \\ x^2+8x+16=-7+16&amp;\text{ Step 3 }&amp; \\ (x+4)^2=9&amp;\text{ Step 4 }&amp; \\ x+4=3&amp;\text{ Step 5 }&amp; \\ x=-1&amp;\text{ Step 6 }&amp;\end{array} \)</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the error when completing the square:</para>
  <para>Work through the steps on your own and compare:</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{lc}x^2+8x+7=0&amp;\text{ Step 1 } \\ x^2+8x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 2 } \\ x^2+8x+16=-7+16&amp;\text{ Step 3 } \\ (x+4)^2=9&amp;\text{ Step 4 } \\ x+4=\pm3&amp;\text{ Step 5 } \\ x=-1,x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 6 }\end{array}\)</para>
  <para>The error occurred in Step 5 when the plus-minus sign was not included after taking the square root of each side. This led to the second possible equation not being written.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
